ositive change and  prevent future loss of life  and i like to turn it over to mr michael  ellison  thank you lisa  to begin with i'd like to acknowledge  with gratitude that the death review  panel was convened on the unseated  traditional territory of the coast  salish peoples  illicit drug toxicity is the leading  cause of unnatural death in the province  accounting for more deaths than suicides  homicides  motor vehicle incidents drownings and  fire related deaths combined  before i talk about numbers findings and  recommendations  i want to remind everyone  that we're talking about people  people who resided across british  columbia in communities large and small  people dealing with pain and trauma  people living in poverty or employed  supporting families  people young and old  healthy and unwell  people from diverse ethnic backgrounds  people just like everyone else  the continued toll of unintentional drug  toxicity deaths has created devastating  impacts  on families friends and communities of  the deceased  to provide some perspective since the  public health emergency was declared  more people have died than the  populations of revelstoke kitimat  merit  hope or cumberland  more people have died than would fit in  save foods memorial arena in victoria  so how did we get here today  in 2015 illicit drug toxicity deaths  increased considerably over the previous  year  and in 2016 that number almost doubled  and the provincial health officer  declared a public health emergency  the numbers continued to rapidly  increase and in 2017  the chief coroner convened a death  review panel which brought together  experts to look at the bc corner service  data to see if any lessons could be  learned that would reduce the number of  deaths  the 2017 panel identified a lot of  information about who was dying  where the deceased lived challenges they  faced in their lives and most of that  information is held consistent since  that panel and can be found in the bc  corn