e of  years ago  and our final question today comes from  mary brooke island social trend  hi uh thanks for taking uh these  questions today  so many times today you've mentioned  about doing things differently outside  the box  i'm wondering how much your panel  discussed the social social and economic  causes of the despair and the  disenfranchised  disenfranchisement that people  experience which leads them to wanting  to take an escape whether it's just on a  leisure use basis or they become  addicted  and  and with the depth that they may have  discussed which ministries are you  pointing that to  to deal with education about systemic  societal  change and specifically a lot of  exclusion that happens in society and  people feel left out and they kind of  tip over the edge  yeah thank you very much for that  question um  in in thinking outside the box clearly  when we talk about a um  an evidence-based  continuum  it absolutely needs needs to address a  number of things that you're talking  about um  the the uh the whole deprivation piece  so  that's really about bringing together  right from from  a health promotion  um  uh part of the continuum all the way up  to um  intensive treatment services and so  that's going to involve  education that's going to involve  housing it's certainly going to involve  mental health it's going to involve  community agencies  and as as lisa identified and really  couldn't  stress  um  any more supporters strongly enough is  the importance of um the people affected  themselves and their families about  what's going to work for them what do  they need but you're you're um  completely right that  simply providing um a uh  a safer drug supply is not going to  resolve this in and of itself that's  really a key component to to keeping  people um  alive  but  we really need that continuum of support  services uh to help uh people and and  it's it's not for as you point out it's  not simply going to be um  about the uh uh  physical um  um  addiction to to a particular drug  there's there's certainly going to be um  housing and  a number of other things for a number of  people who have some some some other  challenges and those all are going to  need to be addressed if if we're going  to make  meaningful inroads into the quality of  these people's lives  mary do you have a follow-up  yes thank you um so you mentioned today  about there are various points of  contact with physicians nurse  practitioners emergency rooms and  clinics  where engagement and screening could  happen  how much of that is happening now and  what do those practitioners in those  range of settings need  to  start digging into this problem  so  one of the recommendations is about a  practice standard around that and and  further training but  i i think  you've hopefully picked up from some of  my comments today that  those practitioners also  in terms of screening and referral are  going to need services  to to refer people to and those services  need to exist  on that continuum to meet the variety of  needs they need to be  evidence-based they need to be  accessible and they need to exist  that concludes today's event thank you  everybody for joining us today  you
 

